  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{8}{3+4\\text{i}}=($\n {{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$",
  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $3+4\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $3-4\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $3-4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3+4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{8}{3+4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{3-4\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(3-4\\text{i})}{{3}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-32\\text{i}}{9-(-16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-32\\text{i}}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24-32\\text{i}}{25}=(\\frac{24}{25})+(-\\frac{32}{25})\\,\\text{i}\n$$",
